-- #1469's SELF-DRAINING PIN: values of TWO constructors of one record type reach the
-- same `.sx` selection.  The LLVM backend prints the correct answer; WasmGC traps at
-- instantiation with `illegal cast`.  Both constructors place `sx` at the same offset,
-- so `T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT` (#1455) does NOT fire here — deliberately: rejecting
-- this class would also reject five measured programs that work on both backends (see
-- `test/typecheck_error_fixtures/field_variant_offset_agree_control.mdk`), and whether a
-- program of this shape traps depends on WHICH CONSTRUCTOR REACHES THE ACCESS AT RUN
-- TIME, which no declaration-level test at the typecheck seam can see.
--
-- Expected output, DERIVED BY HAND: `mkSA = SA { sx = 1, … }`, `mkSB = SB { sx = 3, … }`,
-- so `t=1,3`.  eval and the native binary both print it; the golden is theirs.
--
-- ⚠️ THE WasmGC ARM IS LEDGERED, NOT SILENT.  `test/engine_divergence.txt` carries this
-- cell as `wasm:codegen-bug #1469`, so `diff_compiler_engines` FAILS with PROMOTE the
-- moment wasm stops trapping — which is what #1465 (tag-dispatched field selectors in
-- both backends) will do.  Delete the row and this comment then.
--
-- MEASURED on cold builds of BOTH the merge base `bfcd4ea7` and this branch, so the
-- divergence is pre-existing and neither introduced nor drained by #1455.
import zshared.{mkSA, mkSB}

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn ("t=" ++ intToString mkSA.sx ++ "," ++ intToString mkSB.sx)
